I assigned this to [~accountid:63d78359a5d0c82630686b2d] because she's usually the one who verifies PodAuto bugs. Feel free to assign to someone else if needed.

We need to verify that the VPA can scale Custom Resources.

1. Here are steps to verify you can use VPA on CR:
 # I attached a bash script called[^test-scalable.sh] and a CustomResourceDefintion yaml file called [^scalablepod-crd.yaml]
 # Make sure you have a cluster and it is installed with vertical-pod-autoscaler-operator 4.1[4,5,6,7,8]
 # 
{code:java}
$ oc project default{code}

 # Make sure you don't have any VPAs
{code:java}
$ oc delete vpa -A
{code}

 # Make sure the bash script and yaml are in the same directory.
 # From that same directory, run *chmod +x test-scalable.sh*
 # From the same directory, run *./test-scalable.sh 10*
 # In a separate terminal, watch the VPAs to make sure they are recommending values to those pods: $ {*}watch o{*}{*}c get vpa -A{*}
 # *Wait at least 1 minute.*
 # After waiting at least a minute, you should find that the pods have been evicted by the VPA updater. You can check events by running 
{code:java}
$ oc get events | grep EvictedByVPA {code}

 # But since there is no controller to managing the CustomResource, the pods will not get restarted. Restart the pods by running the same script again with the same argument. 
{code:java}
$ ./test-scalable.sh 10 {code}

 # Pods requests should be updated with new numbers supplied by VPA.

{code:java}
$ oc get pod scalable-cr-1  -o custom-columns=CONTAINER:.spec.containers[0].name,REQUESTS:.spec.containers[0].resources.reques
ts {code}

Side note: If you inspect the script, it is supposed to apply the CustomResourceDefintion, apply a VPA, apply a CustomResource, and applies pods that are "managed" by the CustomResource.

